# Menu responsiveness - 2026-10-26

The recurring part of the reported stutter is reproducible with the production menu and saved paint on all seven cars. Repeated Garage visits resumed expensive native captures for cards outside the scrolling window. Settings/Profile also reconstructed the unchanged native header car.

MR01 retains the same header control across shell changes and Settings/Profile returns, calls its existing refresh path, and replaces it for another records owner. MR02 checks the intersection of the thumbnail, viewport and every clipping ancestor before reading or rendering a customized picture. Leaving the page cancels its temporary capture immediately. Capture construction uses the existing cooperative car builder and waits for complete geometry before publishing. Existing identity, stale-result, disk bounds and account fences remain.

MR03 prepares the original workshop architecture offline in `game/assets/garage/workshop_v1.scn`; first entry loads those native resources instead of generating and batching the room during navigation. The room layout, materials, furnishings, moving record elements, floor and live reflection ownership are preserved. The resource is approximately 2.5 MB compressed. The independent regression compares every prepared node transform, mesh surface array and authored finish against a fresh procedural build. Source provenance accompanies the asset. Regenerate with the pinned engine and `res://tests/bake_garage_architecture.gd` after architecture changes, then run the geometry regression.

The runtime delta is confined to `game/core/app.gd`, `game/ui/car_showroom.gd`, `game/ui/garage_environment.gd` and `game/ui/garage_preview_cache.gd`, plus the prepared resource. Other current App, paint, Rivalry, handling, rendering and track work is preserved. R01 retains persistence/account boundaries; Q01 records cold and warm timing separately.

## Measurements

The read-only profile runs the actual App at 1280?720, with isolated local saves, no configured API, a dummy audio driver and a fresh appearance cache. It samples 80 frames after each of ten transitions. These are desktop Compatibility measurements on the RTX 3090 workstation, not phone measurements or a frame-time guarantee. All raw frames and synchronous dispatch times are in the report.

| Operation | Matched original | Updated |
|---|---:|---:|
| First Garage dispatch | 1188.0 ms | 1078.8 ms |
| Garage revisit 1 worst frame | 2161.4 ms | 25.4 ms |
| Settings dispatch | 228.7 ms | 28.8 ms |
| Garage revisit 2 worst frame | 1137.2 ms | 10.6 ms |

These matched measurements use the original live header. A concurrent change subsequently replaced the header with imported pictures and is preserved/excluded from this patch. In the combined workspace, the first Race dispatch/frame measured 82.8/113.0 ms; Garage revisits measured 437.5 ms and 22.0 ms, with Settings dispatch 35.9 ms. The first Garage draw stalled for 34.6 seconds in that run. These current integration timings retain an occasional visible-preview hitch, and cold first entry remains expensive. This work reduces recurring page-change/capture stalls and room construction; it does not solve cold driver/shader preparation or make Garage instantly ready. The selected car itself still constructs synchronously; no guaranteed first-draw latency is claimed. Non-Compact capture builders can also contain larger unsliced stages when those cars are actually revealed.

## Verification and retained findings

- Focused headless: 35 checks pass for header lifetime and exact Settings/Profile return attachment, records-owner replacement, fixed 88x56 header bounds through navigation and live resizing, viewport/clipping intersection, and prepared geometry/finish equivalence.
- Focused native Compatibility: 42 checks pass, including byte-exact equally warmed synchronous/cooperative native captures. A first-draw fixture initially differed by 16 one-level color components, including between two synchronous captures; the corrected fixture performs an explicit warm-up and retains the zero-difference assertions. Initial sources, captures and failures remain in evidence. Visual review of the combined header found an oversized retained picture; attachment now resets its anchors/offsets against the destination button. Both the control and picture bounds are checked after every return and resize; the original overflow capture is retained.
- Desktop Mobile strict independent-image diagnostic: 32 checks / two failures. The synchronous control differs too: 2,421 color components, maximum 10/255, versus 2,441 and the same maximum for cooperative capture in the recorded diagnostic. These assertions remain intact; there is no Mobile bit-exact image claim. Turning off debanding was tested, had no effect and is not a production change.
- Retained actual App: 141 checks pass. Retained native mobile-audit checks: 266 Compatibility checks pass; 266 desktop Mobile checks pass after the concurrent header images were imported. Parser: 1,283 scripts / zero failures.
- Broader `test_menu_page_swipe.gd`: 162 checks / 75 failures. Replaying the saved task-start sources only in isolated process memory produces the exact same 75 failure labels and counts. The retained test requires a cold Garage neighbor to be prebuilt, contrary to the current first-entry design. Its assertions were not changed.
- Broader `test_menu_quality.gd` stops on a missing hero-title node and reaches the process deadline. The same missing-title error reproduces with the exact saved App source and a newly constructed App instance. Its original cloud/title fixture and assertions remain unchanged. An initial baseline attempt used a precompiled subclass after reloading its base, produced invalid property offsets, and is retained as an invalid diagnostic rather than evidence about production behavior.
- An initial test teardown left Ogg playback resources referenced. The verbose trace identifies audio objects; disabling test-only playback after the profile has loaded resolves that warning. No production audio change is included.

`report.json`, `run_checks.py`, raw logs, baseline sources, staged fixture sources, screenshots and the scoped source patch are in [the evidence directory](evidence/menu-responsiveness-2026-10-26). The prepared binary is identified separately by SHA-256. An integration import exited zero but logged native-extension copy/load errors from the editor; it is not a clean import pass. A test begun during concurrent header-asset creation logged missing-loader errors even though its 266 assertions completed; that run is not treated as clean. After asset import, integration checks were repeated. The normal `scripts/dev.ps1` entry was attempted first: shell execution policy required a process-local bypass, and then its blanket error handler killed Godot on this sandbox's certificate-store error. Verification continued with the exact pinned console executable, isolated `APPDATA`, no API and captured output. That certificate-store error is retained separately from test failures.

Reload/rebuild is needed to use the changes. Owner acceptance of page responsiveness, cold first entry, scrolling, touch/controller input and phone performance remains. No device attachment check or physical-device test, APK/AAB, purchase, authentication, live mutation, deployment, staging, commit or owner-process restart was performed.
